\section{What is FEA?}

\subsection{Definitions}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{FEM (Finite Element Method):} "a numerical procedure to get \emph{approximate} solutions to mathematical equations (usually PDEs) and their boundary conditions that govern a problem of engineering interest."
\item \textbf{FEA (Finite Element Analysis):} the application of FEM to practical engineering problems.
\item Focus of MEGN 324: FEA for \textbf{structural mechanics} (with brief coverage of buckling and frequency analysis).
\item FEA is a standard Computer-Aided Engineering (CAE) tool used throughout industry, government, and academia.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Common Commercial FEA Software}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Abaqus} — world leader, powerful but expensive
\item \textbf{ANSYS} — comparable to Abaqus, also expensive
\item \textbf{COMSOL} — known for multiphysics coupling (structural + electromagnetic + thermal)
\item \textbf{SolidWorks Simulation} — affordable, common in industry
\item Specialized tools: \textbf{FEBio} (open-source, biomechanics), \textbf{ProCast} (casting flow/solidification)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example Governing PDEs}

\textbf{Solid Mechanics} — 3-D steady linear-elastic stress analysis:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \sigma_x}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial \tau_{yx}}{\partial y} + \frac{\partial \tau_{zx}}{\partial z} &= -F_x \\
\frac{\partial \tau_{xy}}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial \sigma_y}{\partial y} + \frac{\partial \tau_{zy}}{\partial z} &= -F_y \\
\frac{\partial \tau_{xz}}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial \tau_{yz}}{\partial y} + \frac{\partial \sigma_z}{\partial z} &= -F_z
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

with constitutive relations

\begin{equation}
\sigma_x = (2G+\lambda)\varepsilon_{xx} + \lambda(\varepsilon_{yy}+\varepsilon_{zz}), \qquad \tau_{xy} = G\,\gamma_{xy}, \quad \text{etc.}
\end{equation}

and strain–displacement relations $\varepsilon_x = \partial u/\partial x$ and
$\gamma_{xy} = \partial u/\partial y + \partial v/\partial x$, where

\begin{equation}
G = \frac{E}{2(1+\nu)}, \qquad \lambda = \frac{4G^2 \nu}{E - 6G\nu}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Fluid Mechanics} — 2-D laminar Newtonian flow:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial v_x}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial v_y}{\partial y} &= 0 \\
\rho\left(\frac{\partial v_x}{\partial t} + v_x \frac{\partial v_x}{\partial x} + v_y \frac{\partial v_x}{\partial y}\right) &= -\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} + \mu\left(\frac{\partial^2 v_x}{\partial x^2} + \frac{\partial^2 v_x}{\partial y^2}\right) + \rho g_x
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Dynamics and Vibrations} — 1-D spring–mass–damper:

\begin{equation}
m\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} + c\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} + k u = F
\end{equation}

Other example PDE domains covered by FEM: heat transfer.

\subsection{How FEM Works}
\begin{itemize}
\item The real domain is split into \textbf{elements} connected at \textbf{nodes} (points).
\item Simple assumed shape/behavior within each element.
\item Elements are connected to approximate the solution of a complex problem.
\item Element dimensionality: \textbf{1D} = line, \textbf{2D} = area, \textbf{3D} = volume.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Modeling Pipeline}

Real World $\rightarrow$ Mathematical Model (choose governing theory/equations)
$\rightarrow$ FEM (solve approximately for displacements, strains, stresses, forces)
$\rightarrow$ Analysis (interpret results, generate practical insight).

\emph{Example insight:} stress in a support bracket exceeds the endurance
limit under moderate wind loading $\Rightarrow$ redesign needed to avoid
fatigue failure.

\subsection{FEA Applications}
\begin{itemize}
\item Lunar excavator blade structural design
\item Total knee replacement implant mechanics (Halloran, Petrella & Rullkoetter, \emph{J. Biomechanics}, 2005)
\item Lifetime prediction modeling of turbine airfoils for advanced power generation (Karaivanov, PhD thesis, Univ. of Pittsburgh, 2009)
\end{itemize}

\section{Steps in Every FE Analysis}

\textbf{First:} Understand the nature/behavior of the physical system
(shape — beam, plate, block, membrane; deformation mode — bending,
twisting, inflation).

\textbf{Next:} Choose the appropriate theory for the mathematical model
(domain, equations, boundary conditions).

\textbf{Then}, the FE modeling process:

\begin{itemize}
\item 1. Create/obtain \textbf{Geometry} (usually from CAD)
\item 2. Select \textbf{Materials} (properties)
\item 3. Define \textbf{Fixtures} (fixed degrees of freedom, i.e. boundary conditions)
\item 4. Define \textbf{External Loads}
\item 5. Discretize geometry with a \textbf{Mesh} of nodes/elements
\item 6. Compute the numerical \textbf{Solution} of the FE model
\item 7. Conduct \textbf{Verification and Validation (V&V)} of the FE model
\item 8. Return to Step 1 and \textbf{Repeat} until V&V is satisfied
\item 9. \textbf{Document} Steps 1–7 in a clear, concise \textbf{Report}
\end{itemize}

\emph{Note: MEGN 324 focuses on the linear-elastic class of problems.}

\subsection{Worked Example: Cantilever Beam (I-beam with hole)}
\begin{itemize}
\item 1. \textbf{Geometry:} 1000 mm I-beam, obtained from SolidWorks, with a hole through the web (not capturable by beam theory alone $\Rightarrow$ 3-D solid mechanics needed).
\item 2. \textbf{Materials:} linear-elastic steel.
\item 3. \textbf{Fixtures:} fixed at the left end.
\item 4. \textbf{External Loads:} applied at the right (free) end.
\item 5. \textbf{Mesh:} tetrahedral finite elements.
\item 6. \textbf{Solution:} z-direction normal stress and y-direction displacement (deflection) computed and plotted.
\item 7. \textbf{V&V:} Is the mesh too coarse for smooth results? Do results agree with Solid Mechanics (MoM) expectations? — \emph{checking this is critical.}
\item 8. \textbf{Repeat:} refine the mesh around the hole (stress concentration region).
\item 9. \textbf{Report:} document Steps 1–7 clearly so results are reproducible.
\end{itemize}

\section{Verification and Validation (V&V)}

\subsection{Verification — "Qualitative", the domain of mathematics}
\begin{itemize}
\item In short: does the FEA program do what it is supposed to do (model set up correctly, no bugs)?
\item \textbf{NASA:} the process for determining whether a product fulfills its established requirements/specifications.
\item \textbf{ASME:} the process of determining that a computational model accurately represents the underlying mathematical model and its solution.
\item Two sub-types: \textbf{Code Verification} (does the code do what it's supposed to?) and \textbf{Calculation Verification} (are you modeling what you intend to?).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Validation — "Quantitative", the domain of physics}
\begin{itemize}
\item In short: how well does the model approximate \emph{reality} (e.g. agreement with a physical test)?
\item \textbf{NASA:} assessment of a planned/delivered system against the sponsor's operational need in the most realistic achievable environment.
\item \textbf{ASME:} the degree to which a model is an accurate representation of the real world for its intended use.
\item \textbf{MITRE:} the degree to which a model accurately represents reality (experiments) within its intended range of use.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Both verification and validation are required} for a trustworthy FE model.

\section{Computer & File Management Recommendations}
\begin{itemize}
\item Mines classroom computers: recommended for all assignments, \textbf{required for exams}. Do not store anything "locally" that cannot be lost — save to the CSM network drive (\texttt{Z:}), cloud, or an external drive.
\item Personal computers: install SolidWorks 2026 and Mathcad Prime 12 (\emph{not} Mathcad 15) — both free.
\item Mines virtual computers ("GPU Desktop"): good option for old/slow/Mac machines; Documents/Desktop sync to the account.
\item Always keep a backup. SolidWorks Simulation runs faster with local (\texttt{C:}) storage.
\item Computer/login/technical issues are \textbf{not} valid excuses for incorrect, missing, or late work.
\end{itemize}

